  "query_preview": "..\nBiggest change from this morning is extending the Flash Flood\nWatch through tomorrow evening for Graham, Greenlee, Cochise,\nand Santa Cruz Counties.\n\nThe main threat will be very heavy rainfall and flash flooding,\nwith gusty winds a secondary threat. There could be a localized\ngust to 50 kts as convection moves off the terrain, but 30-40kts\nare more likely. A deeper dive into the flash flood setup\nshows abundant moisture and a susceptible area. Widespread \n0.5\"+ with localized amounts of 2\"+ fell across a good chunk of\nfar SEAZ, especially Cochise County yesterday. This area will \nbe the most vulnerable. In addition, CAMs show weakly organized \nclusters/single cell with the potential for cell mergers and at\nleast some training/backbuilding as flow remains relatively \nweak. The best chance for training or backbuilding will be\nacross Cochise and Santa Cruz Counties this evening.\n\nAs the mid/upper level low noses into SEAZ from NM/MX,\nconvection could go well into the evening and perhaps into the\novernight. Confidence is low at this time regarding the \novernight, but there is at least some signal in the HREF of \nhigher rainfall totals across central Pima County. In addition,\nat least some higher res solutions now show additional\nconvective development early morning across areas from Tucson\nsouth to Nogales and west toward Sells. There still is not \nenough confidence to expand the Flash Flood Watch west into \neastern/central Pima County, but its is not out of the question \nwe have a flash flood warning or two this evening or even\ntomorrow morning.\n\nAs for the Tucson Metro, confidence is again low on the\nevolution and propagation of storms off the rim this \nevening/overnight. We will likely have storms in the metro, but \nonce again favoring the Catalina Foothills, Oracle/Catalina, the\nfar east side, and south into the Corona De Tucson. We cannot \nrule out localized flash flooding, but probabilities are rather \nlow. The more likely scenario is seeing light to moderate rain \nshowers with embedded storms late evening into overnight/early\nmorning hours as the mid/upper level low begins to provide more\nlift and elevated instability to the area.\n\nMoving into Tuesday still remains a little tricky given how\nactive today will be and how convection lingers into tomorrow\nmorning, but enough CAMS, HREF probs of 3\"+ amounts, and the\nmid/upper level low moving across the CWA is more than enough to\nbump up confidence. So the watch for tomorrow looks good, with\nuncertainty remaining for areas along the watch in Pinal/Pima\nCounty. \n\nAs we move into Wed/Thu/Fri, there should be a noticeable\ndowntrend in daily showers/storms. While it will trend drier,\nthere will still be daily convection, most likely favoring the\ntypical areas south and east of Tucson. The flash flood threat\nwill also lessen and allow for soils to recover as we heat up.\nWhile the flash flood threat trends down, the threat for gusty\nwinds will increase with better mid and upper level flow/drier \nair. \n\nMonsoon activity looks to ramp back up for the weekend as the\nhigh moves into a more favorable region and multiple waves look\nto track across the area. \n\nDaytime temps below average early this week, transitioning to\nabove average by midweek. Spotty major heat risk is possible for\nportions of Pima County, generally around the metro and across \nthe western third of the county. In addition, the upper Gila \nRiver Valley has some spotty major heat risk. As of now, \nconfidence is not high enough for an Extreme Heat Watch, due to\nthe limited coverage and possibility of monsoon activity/clouds\nlowering aftn temps. We will monitor for now and message an \noverall upward trend in temps and heat risk by mid week.",
